  ],
  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "LOWER leaves letters above U+FFFF alone, and a combining mark stops the final sigma",
  "statement": "LOWER did not change the Deseret capital U+10400, a cased letter above U+FFFF, either alone or inside other text: LOWER(\"A𐐀Σ\") is \"a𐐀ς\", where the Greek capital sigma at the end becomes the final form. Greek sigma depends on what follows: followed by a full stop it takes the final form ς, and followed by a combining mark, U+0300 or U+0301, it takes the medial form σ. PROPER treats a character above U+FFFF as a word boundary, so PROPER(\"a𐐀b\") is \"A𐐀B\", with the B capitalised and the Deseret letter unchanged.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "6 recorded LOWER and 4 recorded PROPER readings, in a workbook at compatibility version 2.",
  "inferred": "The readings cover one Deseret letter and two combining marks. That every letter above U+FFFF and every mark behave the same is inferred.",
  "documentation": null,
